“The project I find most interesting is Cheque Bounce Management.

The core problem wasn't workflow — it was accountability for money that had already been lost. A bounced cheque moves between Cashier, Segregator and Sales Officer, and every handoff creates a period where somebody is responsible for it.

My strongest design decision was the inverted blocker. When A assigns work to B, I create a pending acknowledgement interval. If B doesn't acknowledge within 30 minutes, A gets blocked, not B. The reasoning is that B has little incentive to accept the work, while A has a strong incentive to get it accepted. So I turned an organizational accountability problem into a database predicate.

The second difficult part was payment verification. A collection can contain cash, cheque, UPI and NEFT, and each payment is verified independently. That means a collection can be partially verified, so editability can't be a boolean. It's a matrix of collection verification state × payment state. If previously verified money is edited, its verification is reset, while bank-confirmed UPI/NEFT remains trusted.

I also enforced financial rules such as allowing short-close only when the remaining amount is within the ₹500 bounce charge, while never allowing principal to be written off.

The other technically interesting part was GST e-invoicing, where I had to derive a GST-inclusive breakup that satisfied the government's rounding constraints exactly to the paise.”

